  "student_level": "intermediate",
  "prerequisites": [
    "Euclidean geometry (angle relationships, polygon properties, geometric constructions)",
    "basic abstract algebra (groups, fields, homomorphisms)",
    "linear algebra fundamentals (transformations, matrix representation)",
    "introductory combinatorics and graph theory",
    "proof-writing experience (direct proof, contradiction, induction)",
    "comfort with mathematical notation and definitions"
  ],
  "exit_criteria": [
    "State and apply all seven Huzita-Justin axioms and explain why origami constructions are strictly more powerful than compass-and-straightedge",
    "Determine whether a single-vertex crease pattern is flat-foldable using Kawasaki's and Maekawa's theorems, and enumerate its valid mountain-valley assignments",
    "Explain tree theory and use circle-river packing to design a uniaxial origami base for a given stick figure",
    "Analyze rigid foldability of degree-4 vertex patterns and derive the kinematic properties of Miura-ori including its Poisson's ratio",
    "State and sketch the proof that flat-fold MV assignment is NP-hard, and explain the significance of flat origami's Turing completeness",
    "Use computational tools (Origami Simulator, TreeMaker, Tachi's software) to design, simulate, and verify crease patterns",
    "Read and critically engage with primary literature in origami mathematics (Hull, Demaine, Lang, Tachi)",
    "Identify connections between origami mathematics and prior studies in category theory, optimal transport, and combinatorics"
  ],
